ved in the oppression and at  some point even the killing of so many  protesters I do appreciate the views  that are expressed by our young people  remember these are young people who were  subjected to all kind of oppression you  lived through that and you know it in  the streets and all this for years for  decades so that's why they have disputes  but I think gradually we are building  this partnership together none of this  equation two sides of the equation  will be able to move on their own  to build this new nation appreciate  those views but we are working together  to address them let's turn to the topic  of the the you know the clearing of the  sit-in in June and the people that were  killed on the streets a lot of them  believe that the rapid support forces  were involved in this attack when will  these people see justice because so far  some say the results just have not been  up to what happened to the  protesters on the streets I'm sure you  know so well and the constitutional  document which is guiding this  transitional period has a very clear  undertaking that will establish an  investigation committee so let us not  jump to conclusion that investigation  committee formed from very respected  lawyers and people in the legal field in  Sudan they started their work over three  months now and they're doing their work  let them do their work and produce the  report you recently requested that UN  peacekeeping forces be deployed in the  hold of Sudan why did you do that  why is the UN needed now in Sudan  actually let us put this in into its  proper context we did not just walk up  one day and say we want the UN to come  to the country the UN is there I'm not  calling it the UN is there under Chapter  7 meaning that is if there is anything  that you could closely call it loss of  sovereignty is what is happening today  what we are calling for is to move the  UN from chapter 7 chapter 6 which would  allow us to express our wish and if you  could call it anywhere dictate on the UN  on our priorities this is where we would  like to create an opportunity where we  tell the UN this is what we want I'd  like you to come do one two three four  on our teams the whole country approach  is an idea we discussed in our National  Committee for a number of months what it  tells us in the past the UN intervention  in Sudan was compartmentalized it is  therefore it is a Blue Nile it is a new  mountains South Kordofan and all that I  would like to see when we see whole  country approach is addressing the  challenges and the issues and the  requirement and objectives of the entire  transitional period  this certainly has some agenda which is  of national debt of whole country when  you talk about elections I'm not going  to have them in there for could be the  whole country Constitution conference it  will engulf the entire country the  process itself we would like to enlist  the support of the UN in those processes  that of a national nature that's why we  call it a whole country approach that  support could be providing one advisor  could be providing technical support  take an example the election today we  would like to embark on a process that  will conclude this transitional period  with an election the UN has accumulated  experiences technical and otherwise  across the globe support on addressing  issues of election systems an election  system that will provide us with an  opportunity to represent all our people  the one could provide that so it's not  about entrusting this entirely to the UN  the funding of the elections the UN has  accumulated various experience in  generating resources and funds to  support the elections so it's only  perfect and we are part of the Union  it's not like another body you were  something that's coming from somewhere  let's turn to the topic of the hour the  possibility of former president Alma del  Bashir to appear before the  International Criminal Court will he be  sent over to the Hague the idea of  justice need to be served to the maximum  satisfaction of the victim is something  was well articulated in the  constitutional transitional period  document we will definitely uphold that  there are four ways of addressing this  the ICC is one of them ICC itself has so  many levels it could be an ICC in the  heck could be an ICC  planned court that is Sudan or in the  region transitional justice issues of  Truth and Reconciliation compensation  individual and community we are not  going to rest until the victims those  victims to be satisfied that just is not  only being perceived but actually being  served and that's how we see it  from what I understand all options are  now open you still haven't decided  whether it be in The Hague or in Sudan  or in a Regional Court of course this is  something you know the the announcement  of this was part of our peace  negotiations and I think this is  something we will I want to tell you one  thing  any person who committed atrocities  criminal financial we will have to be  trained and that's our bottom line we're  working on rebuilding your judicial  system and all that and we hope to have  a credible independent judicial system  Sudanese let's talk about Germany and  your visit to Germany the past couple of  days have been quite historic in Sudan  Germany relations concretely what how  can Germany help Sudan in the meeting  with the Chancellor Her Excellency  Angela Merkel yesterday provide an  opportunity for us to reflect discuss in  depth or interest or mutual interest for  that matter and we agreed on concrete  areas that German will work with us  supporting us whether it is the economy  the energy sector vocational training  mining agriculture so many hours and  we're very delighted  this is signaling a new beginning of  this relationship which has very high  promise and we look forward to this  cooperation that would also allow other  partners and Friends  and nations to follow suit what the  gentleman is doing  you mentioned the economy Sudan badly  needs foreign investment what are your  plans to attract foreign investment to  Sudan your government's plans Sudan is a  very rich country we don't want to live  on handouts but so rich in the sense  that if we were able to create the right  climate meaning getting it right in the  legal sphere independent judiciary  system respect for property rights and  all that and we are working on this  creating a one-stop shop for investors  creating an environment where a serious  genuine investors come work with us  pay their taxes and be able to  repatriate whatever profit they make and  we're creating that environment and  we're very confident we'll get there  Sudan is surrounded by many countries  that have seen change because of popular  uprisings and many of them have  descended into chaos  Sudan different absolutely Sudan is  different in the sense that we are in  this middle as you mention it whether  you call it the sorry all in the center  of Africa Sudan change is unique in many  respects is peaceful  it has great hopes for the entire region  I call it getting it right in Sudan has  a strategic impact and effect you can  imagine the spillover effect in the  entire region but in the north to our  west south and east what we would like  to see our friend and partners working  with us making this a success story it  has all the right ingredients of making  it a success in the sea of misery and  conflict around us that's why it is a  unique experiment unique in nature and  all that and let us get friends and  partners what  with us the world and in that particular  part of the world is looking for success  stories that were absent for a long  period of time Sudan is giving that hope  is giving that opportunity let us grab  it let us work together on it and make  it the real success story that will  change the face of that birth tuft Prime  Minister thank you very much for your  time  oh it's gonna be very much thank you

